{"content":[{"c":["RIDDLE"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Word puzzle widely used and esteemed in the ancient world, both as an everyday amusement and as a test of wisdom at a more serious level. The point of a riddle was the discovery of a concealed meaning. Riddles, therefore, may be broadly distinguished from fables, which, like Jotham’s celebrated plant fable (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.9.7-15","r":"Judg.9.7-15","rawR":"Judg.9.7-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.9.7-15","value":"Judg.9.7-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jgs 9:7-15"},"), contained an easily discerned significance. Obviously, there is an intermediate area where there is no sharp differentiation. For instance, Ezekiel’s riddle (",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.17.1-24","r":"Ezek.17.1-24","rawR":"Ezek.17.1-24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.17.1-24","value":"Ezek.17.1-24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ez 17"},") has sometimes been classified as a plant fable."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Samson’s riddle at his wedding feast is the best-known biblical riddle (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.14.1-20","r":"Judg.14.1-20","rawR":"Judg.14.1-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.14.1-20","value":"Judg.14.1-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jgs 14"},"). Probably it was a form of diversion used on such occasions (vv ",{"href":"?bref=Judg.14.12-13","r":"Judg.14.12-13","rawR":"Judg.14.12-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.14.12-13","value":"Judg.14.12-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"12-13"},"). The riddle took the form of a couplet: “From the one who eats came something to eat; out of the strong came something sweet” (v ",{"href":"?bref=Judg.14.14","r":"Judg.14.14","rawR":"Judg.14.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.14.14","value":"Judg.14.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"14"},"). Samson’s 30 young men threatened his betrothed, who wheedled the secret from him: “What is sweeter than honey? What is stronger than a lion?” (v ",{"href":"?bref=Judg.14.18","r":"Judg.14.18","rawR":"Judg.14.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.14.18","value":"Judg.14.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"18"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["Solomon’s wisdom was demonstrated in his ability to answer the “hard questions” (lit., “riddles”) of the queen of Sheba (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.10.1-4","r":"1Kgs.10.1-4","rawR":"1Kgs.10.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.10.1-4","value":"1Kgs.10.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 10:1-4"},"). His reputation for this kind of wisdom is further demonstrated by Ben Sirach: “Your soul covered the earth, and you filled it with parables and riddles” (",{"href":"?bref=Ecclus.47.15","r":"Ecclus.47.15","rawR":"Ecclus.47.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ecclus.47.15","value":"Ecclus.47.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ecclus 47:15"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["Josephus notes a contest of wits between Solomon and Hiram, with riddles being exchanged. Solomon won the earlier exchanges, but Hiram finally outwitted him by enlisting outside help (",{"c":["Antiquities"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," 8.5.5). Such wisdom to solve riddles was, understandably, claimed by Israel’s wise men (e.g., ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.49.4","r":"Ps.49.4","rawR":"Ps.49.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.49.4","value":"Ps.49.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 49:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.1.6","r":"Pr.1.6","rawR":"Pr.1.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.1.6","value":"Pr.1.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prv 1:6"},"). In ",{"href":"?bref=Dan.8.23-24","r":"Dan.8.23-24","rawR":"Dan.8.23-24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Dan.8.23-24","value":"Dan.8.23-24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Daniel 8:23-24"}," there is the apocalyptic vision of “a master of intrigue [who] will rise to power” (lit. “one who understands riddles”). Daniel himself possessed the same ability to “interpret dreams, explain riddles, and solve difficult problems” (",{"href":"?bref=Dan.5.12","r":"Dan.5.12","rawR":"Dan.5.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Dan.5.12","value":"Dan.5.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dn 5:12"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["In the NT riddles figure infrequently. The various “hard sayings” of Jesus (e.g., ",{"href":"?bref=John.6.60","r":"John.6.60","rawR":"John.6.60","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.6.60","value":"John.6.60"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jn 6:60"},") are difficult to accept and are equally as hard to understand. Possibly the only true riddle is the number of the beast, 666 (",{"href":"?bref=Rev.13.18","r":"Rev.13.18","rawR":"Rev.13.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.13.18","value":"Rev.13.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rv 13:18"},"). Various attempts have been made to identify a person, after the pattern of numerical references in contemporary literature. Of these, the emperor Nero is the most plausible candidate."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Riddle","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Jgs 9:7-15","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.9.7-15","rawQueryValue":"Judg.9.7-15","sourceRefExact":"Judg.9.7-15"},{"anchorText":"Ez 17","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ezek.17.1-24","rawQueryValue":"Ezek.17.1-24","sourceRefExact":"Ezek.17.1-24"},{"anchorText":"Jgs 14","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.14.1-20","rawQueryValue":"Judg.14.1-20","sourceRefExact":"Judg.14.1-20"},{"anchorText":"12-13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.14.12-13","rawQueryValue":"Judg.14.12-13","sourceRefExact":"Judg.14.12-13"},{"anchorText":"14","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.14.14","rawQueryValue":"Judg.14.14","sourceRefExact":"Judg.14.14"},{"anchorText":"18","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.14.18","rawQueryValue":"Judg.14.18","sourceRefExact":"Judg.14.18"},{"anchorText":"1 Kgs 10:1-4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Kgs.10.1-4","rawQueryValue":"1Kgs.10.1-4","sourceRefExact":"1Kgs.10.1-4"},{"anchorText":"Ecclus 47:15","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ecclus.47.15","rawQueryValue":"Ecclus.47.15","sourceRefExact":"Ecclus.47.15"},{"anchorText":"Ps 49:4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ps.49.4","rawQueryValue":"Ps.49.4","sourceRefExact":"Ps.49.4"},{"anchorText":"Prv 1:6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Pr.1.6","rawQueryValue":"Pr.1.6","sourceRefExact":"Pr.1.6"},{"anchorText":"Daniel 8:23-24","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Dan.8.23-24","rawQueryValue":"Dan.8.23-24","sourceRefExact":"Dan.8.23-24"},{"anchorText":"Dn 5:12","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Dan.5.12","rawQueryValue":"Dan.5.12","sourceRefExact":"Dan.5.12"},{"anchorText":"Jn 6:60","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=John.6.60","rawQueryValue":"John.6.60","sourceRefExact":"John.6.60"},{"anchorText":"Rv 13:18","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Rev.13.18","rawQueryValue":"Rev.13.18","sourceRefExact":"Rev.13.18"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c21375dab074282ad2ce6a11b1eb0462eaa02a8fbc62dd457cf2b81f9cf8286c","sourceItemName":"Riddle","sourceItemOrder":147,"sourceMember":"Articles/R.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Riddle","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Riddle","title":"Riddle","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}